1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,568
Welcome to Unit Ten, the last unit of
Algebra One: radicals and data. In this

2
00:00:05,568 --> 00:00:08,171
lesson, we'll simplify square roots.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:14,537
The key rule: the square root of a times b
equals the square root of a, times the

4
00:00:14,537 --> 00:00:15,636
square root of b.

5
00:00:16,086 --> 00:00:21,331
So look for perfect square factors, like
four, nine, sixteen, twenty-five and

6
00:00:21,331 --> 00:00:22,081
thirty-six.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,227
Simplify the square root of fifty.

8
00:00:25,677 --> 00:00:31,084
Fifty is twenty-five times two. So it's
the square root of twenty-five, times the

9
00:00:31,084 --> 00:00:33,954
square root of two, which is five root
two.

10
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:40,114
Variables work the same way. The square
root of x squared is x, for x not

11
00:00:40,114 --> 00:00:40,732
negative.

12
00:00:41,182 --> 00:00:47,312
The square root of eighteen x cubed is the
square root of nine x squared times two x,

13
00:00:47,312 --> 00:00:49,331
which is three x root two x.

14
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:54,776
To multiply radicals, multiply what's
inside, then simplify.

15
00:00:55,226 --> 00:01:00,905
Root six times root ten is root sixty,
which is root four times fifteen, or two

16
00:01:00,905 --> 00:01:01,839
root fifteen.

17
00:01:02,967 --> 00:01:09,131
A simplified answer has no radical in the
denominator. To fix one, multiply top and

18
00:01:09,131 --> 00:01:10,839
bottom by that radical.

19
00:01:11,289 --> 00:01:14,361
Three over root two becomes three root two
over two.

20
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,636
Your turn. Simplify the square root of
seventy-two.

21
00:01:19,086 --> 00:01:20,622
Pause the video and try it.

22
00:01:21,072 --> 00:01:25,531
Seventy-two is thirty-six times two, so
the answer is six root two.

23
00:01:26,666 --> 00:01:32,511
Adding radicals works a lot like combining
like terms. In this lesson, we'll see how.

24
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:36,449
Like radicals have the same number under
the root.

25
00:01:36,899 --> 00:01:43,659
Three root five plus two root five is five
root five, just like three x plus two x is

26
00:01:43,659 --> 00:01:44,216
five x.

27
00:01:45,366 --> 00:01:50,379
Sometimes you need to simplify first. Root
twelve plus root twenty-seven.

28
00:01:50,829 --> 00:01:55,722
Root twelve is two root three, and root
twenty-seven is three root three.

29
00:01:55,722 --> 00:01:57,464
Together, five root three.

30
00:01:58,599 --> 00:02:03,165
You can distribute too. Root two times the
quantity three plus root eight.

31
00:02:03,615 --> 00:02:08,180
That's three root two plus root sixteen,
which is three root two plus four.

32
00:02:09,299 --> 00:02:14,430
Radicals appear when you use the
Pythagorean theorem. A right triangle has

33
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:15,678
legs two and four.

34
00:02:16,128 --> 00:02:21,923
The hypotenuse is the square root of four
plus sixteen, which is root twenty, or two

35
00:02:21,923 --> 00:02:22,613
root five.

36
00:02:23,733 --> 00:02:27,167
Watch out: root two plus root three is not
root five.

37
00:02:27,617 --> 00:02:29,815
You can only combine like radicals.

38
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:34,154
Your turn. Simplify root eighteen plus
root fifty.

39
00:02:34,604 --> 00:02:36,140
Pause the video and try it.

40
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:42,313
Root eighteen is three root two, and root
fifty is five root two. The total is eight

41
00:02:42,313 --> 00:02:42,926
root two.

42
00:02:44,066 --> 00:02:49,677
Now we turn to statistics. In this lesson,
we'll describe a data set with its center

43
00:02:49,677 --> 00:02:50,679
and its spread.

44
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:56,301
The mean is the average: add the values
and divide by how many there are.

45
00:02:56,751 --> 00:03:02,467
For two, four, four, five and ten, the sum
is twenty-five, and there are five values,

46
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:03,812
so the mean is five.

47
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:08,453
The median is the middle value when the
data is in order.

48
00:03:08,903 --> 00:03:14,469
For two, four, four, five, ten, the median
is four. With an even number of values,

49
00:03:14,469 --> 00:03:16,369
average the two middle ones.

50
00:03:17,499 --> 00:03:22,065
The range is the largest minus the
smallest: ten minus two is eight.

51
00:03:22,515 --> 00:03:27,600
The interquartile range, or IQR, is the
spread of the middle half: the third

52
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,875
quartile minus the first quartile.

53
00:03:30,999 --> 00:03:37,167
An outlier is a value far from the rest. A
common rule flags anything more than one

54
00:03:37,167 --> 00:03:39,917
and a half IQRs beyond the quartiles.

55
00:03:40,367 --> 00:03:44,185
Outliers pull the mean toward them, but
barely move the median.

56
00:03:45,333 --> 00:03:50,261
So for skewed data or data with outliers,
use the median and IQR.

57
00:03:50,711 --> 00:03:56,102
For symmetric data, the mean and standard
deviation work well. Standard deviation

58
00:03:56,102 --> 00:03:59,031
measures the typical distance from the
mean.

59
00:04:00,166 --> 00:04:04,554
Your turn. Find the mean and median of
three, seven, eight, nine and

60
00:04:04,554 --> 00:04:05,393
twenty-three.

61
00:04:05,843 --> 00:04:07,379
Pause the video and try it.

62
00:04:07,829 --> 00:04:12,476
The sum is fifty, so the mean is ten. The
median is eight. The outlier,

63
00:04:12,476 --> 00:04:14,570
twenty-three, pulls the mean up.

64
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:21,012
In this last lesson of Algebra One, we'll
display data so its shape is easy to see.

65
00:04:22,133 --> 00:04:26,080
A dot plot puts one dot above the number
line for each value.

66
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:28,578
It works well for small data sets.

67
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:35,206
A histogram groups values into equal-width
intervals, and draws a bar for how many

68
00:04:35,206 --> 00:04:36,079
fall in each.

69
00:04:36,529 --> 00:04:40,561
Its shape shows if the data is symmetric,
or skewed to one side.

70
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:47,278
The five-number summary is the minimum,
first quartile, median, third quartile and

71
00:04:47,278 --> 00:04:47,823
maximum.

72
00:04:48,273 --> 00:04:53,980
For one, three, four, six, seven, eight,
ten, eleven and fifteen, that's one, three

73
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:57,211
and a half, seven, ten and a half, and
fifteen.

74
00:04:58,333 --> 00:05:00,744
A box plot draws those five numbers.

75
00:05:01,194 --> 00:05:06,608
The box runs from the first to the third
quartile, with a line at the median. The

76
00:05:06,608 --> 00:05:09,215
whiskers reach the minimum and maximum.

77
00:05:10,333 --> 00:05:12,595
Box plots make comparisons easy.

78
00:05:13,045 --> 00:05:17,973
If one class's box sits higher and is
narrower, that class scored higher, and

79
00:05:17,973 --> 00:05:19,125
more consistently.

80
00:05:20,267 --> 00:05:25,877
Your turn. In a box plot, the box runs
from twelve to twenty. What is the IQR?

81
00:05:26,327 --> 00:05:27,863
Pause the video and try it.

82
00:05:28,313 --> 00:05:29,977
Twenty minus twelve is eight.

83
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:36,122
That completes Unit Ten, and all of
Algebra One. Congratulations! Next, we'll

84
00:05:36,122 --> 00:05:41,013
start Algebra Two. If this helped,
subscribe to School Math, Explained, and

85
00:05:41,013 --> 00:05:44,796
turn on notifications so you don't miss
it. See you there.

